  ### The real question is not whether AI is already in the house. The question is: which room is it busy redecorating?
  
  We all know that #AI is reshaping work in profound ways (and also that the disruptions have surely only just begun).
  
  A recent book called Bitcoin One Million by entrepreneurs/tech strategists Fred Krueger\* and Ben Sigman makes a somber claim: “Your profession has an expiration date.”  It even includes a table posting the actual year when they predictnine professions will “die” – killed off by AI – and citing a couple of supporting assumptions for each argument.
  
  \\* Surely not THAT Freddy Krueger!
  
  The gist of the table is that we better buckle up, because nobody’s got much time left!
  
  ### Could these dates be right?
  
  What should we think about all this?  It dawned on me that since I named this newsletter "What If…", it would behoove me to take a closer look at these projections and offer some commentary.  So here goes.
  
  Right off the bat, let’s stipulate that timetables for when entire professions will disappear are more art than science. (Or, from the standpoint of promoting a book: more hype than reality.)  But the uncomfortable fact remains that we don’t really know.  There’s bound to be lots of displacement thanks to AI.  But will entire professions “die”??  That seems like a stretch.
  
  Below, I briefly unpack each of Krueger & Sigman’s nine “deaths foretold.”  I start by restating the timeline’s claim and repeating verbatim the authors’ own bullet points, before adding my own remarks in response.
  
  I would love to have your feedback, so please chime in!  Without further ado:
  
  * * *
  
  ### 1\. The profession that will die: Driving.  When: 2029
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - Self-driving becomes ubiquitous.
  - 12 million drivers unemployed.
  
  Commentary: Completely autonomous vehicles on public roads could be widespread by 2029, but “ubiquitous” seems unlikely.  There will still be regulatory, liability, and infrastructure challenges to deal with. Even if Level 4/5 autonomy scales, adoption will likely vary by region and use case.  For example, long-haul trucking might get there well before urban passenger fleets.  Job losses will be real, but likely phased. New roles will emerge, too, such as remote fleet supervisors, data auditors, and so on.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 2\. The profession that will die: Doctors.  When: 2030
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - AI diagnoses better than humans.
  - Prescribes perfectly, never forgets.
  
  Commentary: AI diagnostic tools can match or exceed human accuracy in specific, data-rich domains such as imaging. Yet medicine involves human judgment when there is uncertainty, when patient values have to be considered, and other areas where real live clinicians excel.  (Maybe not all of them, but we would hope the vast majority.)  AI will no doubt become a powerful decision support tool, boosting doctors’ productivity, but it’s unlikely to fully replace them as early as 2030 – certainly not across all domains of medicine.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 3\. The profession that will die: Surgeons.  When: 2032
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - Robotic surgery with zero tremor.
  - Perfect precision every time.
  
  Commentary: Advanced robotic surgery systems already improve precision; zero tremor is a reality. However, surgical outcomes depend on more than precision: judgment, adaptability, and patient care matter, too!  AI-assisted surgical systems will enhance capabilities, but human surgeons will likely remain necessary for complex, novel, or high-risk cases.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 4\. The profession that will die: Coders.  When: 2028
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - AI writes and tests all code.
  - Deploys and maintains systems.
  
  Commentary: The authors think this one will be the quickest to go. Just two years from now. (The list of nine professions does not seem to be in any order.) Large language models already assist with code generation today, and AI will continue to transform programming work. But writing all code and maintaining all systems autonomously by 2028 underestimates the complexity of software ecosystems and human oversight needs. Most realistic scenarios envisage hybrid roles, such as AI-augmented developers, rather than outright replacement.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 5\. The profession that will die: Teachers.  When: 2029
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - Personalized AI tutors available.
  - Individual attention for every student.
  
  Commentary: AI tutors tailored to individual learning styles are already in pilot programs on schools and universities worldwide.  Adaptive learning platforms will expand this penetration over the next few years, but human educators will remain essential because there’s so much more to the profession than just the textbook stuff: social, emotional, and cultural objectives are also important. I would see a future classroom co-facilitated by AI and humans but not dominated by one or the other.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 6\. The profession that will die: Lawyers.  When: 2031
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - AI reads all case law instantly.
  - Writes perfect contracts and briefs.
  
  Commentary: Yes, AI will be indispensable for legal research and drafting standardized contracts. However, litigation strategy, negotiation, and ethical judgment are more nuanced and probably hard to automate.  If AI can handle routine tasks, the future may see legal professionals emphasizing strategic and interpersonal skills.  But still around!
  
  * * *
  
  ### 7\. The profession that will die: Artists.  When: 2030
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - AI generates any creative content.
  - Images, songs, movies on demand.
  
  Commentary: AI already generates tons of “artistic” slop.  (A good example is right at the top of this article!)  Yes, some of it can be quite entertaining, but truly compelling music, images, or video? Scarce as a masterpiece on demand. That’s because creativity is about much more than just output. Intention/purpose, cultural context, message and “higher meaning” can make a huge qualitative difference.
  
  AI art also raises the question of value.  Here I concur with the much-missed Scott Adams who said that one of the reasons we marvel at a work of art is because we’re amazed that a human being could be so talented. How did Michelangelo/M.C Escher/Simon & Garfunkel create something so beautiful? Even if the end product is exactly the same, knowing that an AI-made work of art was just created by a machine will never make us feel the same way. We might still like it, but overall, our reaction is likely to be… >…Yawn…<.  And would anybody pay money for a machine-made Renoir? (Even a Jackson Pollock?)  I think we should expect abundant AI-powered creation tools, but never the demise of art itself.  Or artists.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 8\. The profession that will die: Factory workers.  When: 2031
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - Total automation takes over.
  - “Lights out” manufacturing everywhere.
  
  Commentary: Automation will definitely continue to expand, especially in logistics and any kind of repetitive assembly work. “Lights out” factories (meaning no human presence required) should be technically possible, in certain controlled environments. However, complex assembly, quality control, and exception handling still require human adaptability. Automation will transform work rather than eliminate it outright.
  
  * * *
  
  ### 9\. The profession that will die: Soldiers.  When: 2033
  
  “How it will happen”:
  
  - AI controlled drones and robots.
  - Automated warfare deployed.
  
  Commentary: Although autonomous systems are already used in surveillance and logistics, fully autonomous lethal systems raise major ethical and legal challenges. Global norms and treaties may slow or constrain deployment of fully autonomous weapons, but we can be sure that while this is being worked out, AI will certainly be deeply embedded in defense.
  
  * * *
  
  ## What I think of this timeline
  
  It’s provocative, all right — but no more than a useful starting point for discussion.  What these dates might more accurately reflect is when technical capability will exist, not when society, law, markets, etc. will actually catch up with the technological opportunity and adopt the changes, at scale. Adoption will always lag capability - due to regulation, public acceptance/friction, and economic incentives.
  
  In practice, I would guess we’re more likely to see hybrid futures: humans working with increasingly powerful AI, rather than being outright replaced by it.
  
  Let’s hope so, anyway!
  
