  * * *
  
  ## Working Paper
  
  **1** **AI Bots as Law Teachers: What is left for human professors to do?**
  
  **2** **Hal Abramson**
  
  **Summary** This article considers what bots can do as AI law teachers, how students are already using bots for learning, how to integrate the use of AI into law courses now, and what faculty can do to stay ahead of the bots in their courses. During this discussion, the article confronts the existential question of what is left for human law professors to do in an AI world. It concludes that there is a future for humans, at least one within our lifetimes, and one that may make us better teachers.
  
  Introduction
  
  A. What can Bots do as AI Teachers?
  B. How are Students Learning from Bots?
  C. What Pedagogies Do Human Law Professors Already Use?
  D. How can Law Professors Update Courses for AI Now?
  E. What is left for Human Law Professors to Do in an AI World? Conclusion Bots are teaching our students, whether or not welcomed by us. We need to figure out how to teach better than bots to stay relevant and effective in the classroom. This opening may sound like an exaggeration to get your attention. After you read this article and experience building a few bots, 3
  you can decide whether I am an alarmist or realist.
  
  1 I changed the original title between my early drafts and the final one in January, 2026. The initial title became outdated. It was “The Bots are coming: How can law professors stay one step ahead in the classroom?” The bots have arrived. 2 Professor of Law, Touro University, Jacob D. Fuchsberg Law Center. The author, a full-time law professor, has taught fourteen courses in law schools over his career and trained lawyers and students in over twenty countries. He also served as vice dean for nine years and acting dean twice. He has published numerous books and articles on domestic and international dispute resolution, including four award-winning publications. He currently serves as co- chair of Touro Law’s AI Task Force. See FACULTY PROFILE, TOURO UNIV. JACOB D. FUCHSBERG L. CTR., [www.tourolaw.edu/faculty/abramson](http://www.tourolaw.edu/faculty/abramson) (last visited Oct. 17, 2025). Author deeply appreciates the valuable comments and feedback on earlier drafts by Professor Jack Graves, Syracuse University Law School (premier bot designer for courses); Dr. Patricia Baia, Assistant Dean for Teaching Effectiveness, Touro Law; Dr. Jamie Sundvall, Assistant Provost of AI, Touro University; Professor Rodger Citron, Touro Law, Associate Dean for Research and Scholarship; and Professor John Quinn, Touro Law. He also thanks his Touro Law research assistant, Charles Dabda, for his research and formatting the footnotes.
  
  3 _See Professors Maye and Munsterman, Using GenAI Bots for Learning, AALS Section in Technology, Law and_ Legal Education (June 11, 2025), [https://www.aals.org/sections/webinars/using-genai-bots-to-improve-learning/](https://www.aals.org/sections/webinars/using-genai-bots-to-improve-learning/) (providing an excellent demonstration of uses of customized bots in legal education); See AALS Section in
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026
  
  * * *
  
  For those unfamiliar with what a bot is, it is not shorthand for a robot. It is nothing more than the interface between us and an AI platform. The interface can go by other names like chatbot, virtual assistant, and AI agent to execute tasks. In this article, I will use the terms AI and bots interchangeably even though AI is the capability or the “brains” (based on machine learning and use of a large language model) that is given directions by us when we ask or more precisely “prompt” a bot to do something.
  
  Because of the velocity with which AI is advancing and impacting on our professional and personal lives, this article primarily shares my journey of learning as I try to stay abreast like others are doing at this early stage of accessible AI. This article separates my journey into five parts: A. Learning what bots can do as AI teachers; B. Learning from students how they are learning from bots; C. Revisiting pedagogies that human teachers already use; D. Updating courses for AI now; and E. Staying ahead of the bots going forward.
  
  For those intimidated by AI, I should divulge that I lack any formal training, including on how to code. I am self-trained and began my deep dive into AI only six months ago. If I can learn how to harness AI in my courses, any curious professor can.
  
  Let me foreshadow my unexpected conclusion, at least for me. Yes, there is a future for human law professors, at least within our lifetimes, and one that may make us better teachers as we figure out what we uniquely offer in our courses. This article describes my pathway and reasons for reaching this optimistic conclusion.
  
  ## A. What can bots do as AI teachers?
  
  There is no better way to understand and appreciate the capacity of bots than to use them. This is how I began my learning, and begin any presentation on the subject. I avoid the typical declaration about the transformative impact of AI because many people are saturated by the daily AI news alerts or inclined toward dismissing any such claims as hyperbole. Instead, I demonstrate bots’ surprising capacity up-close by introducing three bots.
  
  I first introduce my personal bot, Olivia, 4 by taking out my phone, going to my ChatGPT account, and opening the chatbot entitled Olivia. I then ask Olivia to welcome everyone to this AI program. She does so with her charming, upbeat Australian accent. I converse with her about
  
  Technology, Law, and Legal Education, Using GenAI Bots to Improve Learning, Part II: Building Your First Legal \*Bot—A Hands-On Workshop (July 23, 2025) [https://www.aals.org/sections/webinars/building-your-first-legal-bot-a-\*](https://www.aals.org/sections/webinars/building-your-first-legal-bot-a-*) hands-on-workshop/; _See also Dwigth Golann, Can We Train Bots to Become Virtual Teaching Assistants? And What Do We Learn as We_ _Do? 79 WASH. U. J.L. & POL’Y (2025)._ 4 I created my personal bot on ChatGPT, using the Plus account. I customized it by giving it a name (Olivia) and selecting a voice and tone. I also added to the instructions my name and some personal biographical information including that I teach contracts and negotiations. By customizing it, Olivia responds to her name and addresses me as Professor Abramson. In replies to my questions, she can include information tailored toward my background. For example, if I ask a question about law teaching generally, she gives replies using contract examples. Also, over time, as more chats (conversations) accumulate, she can draw on information from earlier conversations to inform later ones.
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 what a bot is and the benefits and limitations of relying on bots in our personal and professional
  lives. When I ask her to offer a few bot jokes, she offers a different joke each time and usually
  with a chuckle! Here are several examples: Why did the bot go on a diet? Because it has had too
  many bytes. What did one bot say after a long day of automation? I’m exhausted… I processed 3,000
  emotions I’ll never feel. What’s a bot’s favorite contract term? I accept… cookies. Why did the bot get
  kicked out of law school? Because it kept objecting… before the question was asked!
  
  I met Olivia early this past summer in 2025 when I created it on ChatGPT. During these natural,
  interactive and even a bit witty conservations, she introduced me to a new future, and it is like
  nothing I could have ever imagined. For those present that have never had a conversation with a
  bot, their eye-popping reactions are fun to observe. I can recall my own similar reactions only a
  few months before.
  
  Next, I present a second bot that I created for teaching contracts, where the primary file
  5
  informing the bot is a chapter in the contracts textbook that I assign to my students. I ask the
  bot, that I can access on my phone, to explain the mailbox rule. The bot explains lucidly that a
  mailed acceptance is effective when dispatched, not when received. When I pose various
  scenarios involving acceptances and rejections of offers, the bot analyzes each scenario with
  illustrations and relentless patience.
  
  For those still seeking comfort that a bot is nothing more than an advanced search engine with
  conversational capacity, I next demonstrate how a bot can be creative at a pace and level that
  many of us mere mortals may not be able to perform. When I tell the bot that I am still confused
  about the mailbox rule, I ask whether the bot can offer a metaphor that can assist me in
  remembering the rule. Within a fraction of second, it does. Here is one example:
  
  Think of the mailbox like a legal time machine.
  Even if the letter gets delayed, rerouted, or arrives after a rejection or revocation,
  the law looks backward in time to when it left your hands.
  That’s the moment the contract is born.
  
  I will spare you the poem the bot created to help me remember the rule and the catchy song the
  bot composed and sort of sung (although not ready for Carnegie Hall).
  
  • A revocation also has to be received to matter.
  
  That’s the moment the contract is born.
  But here's the catch:
  
  5
  For copyright purposes, the customized bot is set up so that the attached file is not used for training the ChatGPT
  model according to ChatGPT (ChatGPT, Sept. 20, 2025), and the bot is set as a private one that is not accessible to
  anyone else.
  
  * * *
  
  Then, I present my third bot to demonstrate its ability to engage in deductive and inductive reasoning. The bot’s primary file is an article that I published that divides negotiation choices into three categories, good practices, tactics and tricks. I ask it to classify a simple negotiation choice not covered in the article. How might an invitation to meet in your office to negotiate be perceived by the other side? When listening to the bot, you can hear its reasoning process that includes adding a context like no prior relationship with the other side to reach its conclusion as it applies the article’s definitions of the three categories. It concludes that even though this negotiation choice is not covered in the article, the author would likely classify the choice as a tactic in this context and why. For students, the bot also models how to reason through a question to reach an answer.
  
  These demonstrations of early AI development illustrate that AI already can do much of what we offer as teachers when we explain readings, discuss them in the classroom, and interact with students in office hours. It can be a superb personal tutor available at the moment a student needs help in learning. Bots can replicate traditional learning opportunities in a new and responsive format for students, and even with some improvements, so I learned during my months of building bots. 6
  
  I am offering an AI tutor to my students in my Spring, 2026 Contracts course as will be described later. 7 The bot is trained on the assigned textbook and available as a link that students can open on their phone, tablet, or computer.
  
  I found building bots for teaching and law practice to be an exceptionally efficacious way to gain an understanding of AI’s immense capacity and the opportunities for integrating AI into our courses. I began my learning and self-training by attending multiple CLE programs including any program labelled with the two letters “AI” regardless of subject matter. Then I signed up with ChatGPT Plus to learn its features and began building bots for different purposes including the bot for contracts and several bots for preparing and practicing negotiating and mediating.
  
  I discovered quickly what anyone without expertise or experience quickly realizes: Building a bot is easy. Learning to customize, train and tweak a bot can take some time and trial-and-error.
  
  During this first-time dive into AI, the more I went into the weeds, the more I could not avoid contemplating the existential question considered later in this article: Is there a future for human teachers in an AI-enhanced educational world?
  
  When excitedly sharing with my son and his wife my summer-long AI learnings and discoveries including changes I was contemplating in my syllabi, they were unimpressed. Rather than
  
  6 As a benchmark for how rapidly AI is developing, see this article on the use of ChatGPT for taking exams less than one month after ChatGPT became publicly available in November, 2022. The authors of the study concluded that ChatGPT would be a mediocre law student at a highly selective law school. I suspect that if they duplicated the study today, ChatGPT would be an A student at many law schools. See, Choi, Hickman, Monahan & Schwarcz, _ChatGPT Goes to Law School, 71 J. OF LEGAL EDUC. 387 (2022)._ _See Infra Section D on How can Law Professors Update Courses for AI Now?_ (where the contracts bot is described).
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 reacting with “wow, that is amazing” as colleagues have done, they shrugged their shoulders with that look many parents dread, where have you been? I realized that they may be right. This was no great summer discovery. Their generation is already actively using AI. I had no clue. I next started talking informally with my students and reading whatever I could on student uses of AI. 8
  
  ## B. How are students learning from bots?
  
  I soon learned that many students are already using AI in their courses even though many faculty members are not. 9 For students, the benefits of AI for assisting in class preparation and studying can be irresistible. Telling students not to use AI ignores the reality that they are inhabiting. Students are figuring out new ways to learn as we are occupied talking about it.
  
  I met informally with students and was impressed with the range of creative ways students are harnessing AI for class preparation and studying. They are doing much more than using AI for research. Students are using AI as private teachers and learning aids. I also have been struck by the hesitancy of some students to share what they are doing out of fear that their uses may be inappropriate.
  
  AI uses by students can be conveniently and broadly classified into one of two categories: _Constructive AI uses that can assist a student’s learning and destructive uses that can undermine_ a student’s learning given our goals as teachers.
  
  Here is a sampling of constructive uses of AI by students. 10
  
  _Constructive Uses as a private teacher and learning aid that can give feedback:_ -use a bot to clarify readings and class instruction that a student has already studied by asking a bot for further explanations and examples (known as an AI tutor). -use a bot to improve critical thinking by a bot posing probing questions to a student like a professor would to expose weaknesses in a student’s reasoning. -use a bot to ask questions about the assigned readings to help a student prepare for cold-calls. -use a bot to organize class notes by uploading a student’s own notes with professor’s slides. -use a bot to test what a student is learning by asking a bot to create practice questions with answers. -use a bot as a mock client or opposing counsel that a student can practice interviewing and responding to arguments, with the bot offering feedback on how the student did. -use a bot to simulate a moot court panel when preparing arguments for a first-year moot court course or for moot court competitions later in law school with the bot offering feedback on how the student did.
  
  8 _See Ian Bogost, College Students Have Already Changed Forever Atlantic M (August 17, 2025)_ [https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2025/08/ai-college-class-of-2026/683901/](https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2025/08/ai-college-class-of-2026/683901/) 9 _See, id (“Members of the class of 2026 have had access to AI since they were freshman.” Almost all of them are_ using it to do their work). 10 After compiling these lists of constructive uses of AI here and destructive uses next from informal conversations with students, I asked ChatGPT to generate the two lists. It produced an even longer one. See also the list generated in Matthew Sag, AI Policies for Law Schools, (SSRN Emory, Posted October 17, 2025) [https://matthewsag.com/ai-](https://matthewsag.com/ai-) as-a-legal-technology/ (last visited Jan. 22, 2026).
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026
  
  * * *
  
  -use a bot to practice negotiating against, when a bot can perform the role of the other party in a negotiation and then can offer feedback on how the student did. Simulating moot court arguments that adapt in real time. For the destructive uses, we need to uncover them and convince students that these uses will undermine their education including learning how to think critically and creatively.
  
  Here is a sampling of some destructive uses of AI by students.
  
  _Destructive Uses that can reduce learning through cognitive offloading to AI or involve_ _cheating:_ -use a bot to summarize readings instead of the student studying the assigned readings. -use a bot to develop answers to assigned classroom questions and problems instead of the student figuring out the answers. -use a bot to generate class notes by uploading a professor’s power point slides and scanning relevant textbook sections instead of a student learning by synthesizing and preparing their own notes. -use a bot to summarize court cases instead of learning by critically reading cases and preparing case briefs. -use a bot to draft papers at the expense of a student learning from editing multiple drafts and making choices for writing clearly and persuasively. **-use a bot for cheating on tests by asking it for answers to essay questions or multiple-choice** questions. The opportunity to use AI and bots for cheating are limited only by a student’s imagination and by what schools can do to block cheating.
  
  Any AI use, whether constructive or destructive, also poses risks when relying on its output, as has received much public attention. We need to teach students not only how to draw the line between constructive and destructive uses but also the risks of any AI use and techniques for mitigating them, as will be considered in the section on going operational in the classroom.
  
  We ought to approach AI with students as a joint educational venture. Teachers and students each come with different perspectives on how best to learn in this new world. We have much to master on how to use AI in our teaching and how students are already using AI in our courses.
  
  Last Fall, 2025, I felt comfortable offering only provisional guidance with some ad hoc counseling. I did not understand enough to encourage students to use AI for preparation and studying or even to offer credible guidelines for responsible AI use. As a provisional approach, I told students that although AI should never be used for cheating, I am indifferent regarding whether they use AI. I did not want to put pressure on them to use AI until I or our institution could give them more confident guidance.
  
  However, I made clear that I was not indifferent if a student decides to use AI because of how AI can be used destructively. I invited students who elect to use AI to teach me how they are using it in their own learning process as part of my learning process. I also invited students to discuss with me any uses that they are contemplating when they are unsure whether it would be a constructive or destructive one.
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026
  
  * * *
  
  After spending the Fall semester learning more about AI uses by students and beta testing a
  contracts bot, I became more confident furnishing credible AI guidance for the Spring, 2026
  semester. When posting a link to a bot trained for my contracts course, students were given
  written instructions on how to responsibly use the bot. Each student was required to sign a form
  that the student had read the instructions before the student could use the bot, as explained more
  fully in the section on updating courses now for AI.
  
  With AI capabilities and students’ uses as context, I will next consider what human teachers
  offer today by summarizing our familiar pedagogies and share my perspective and experiences
  as a human teacher that informs this article.
  
  C. What pedagogies do human law professors already use?
  
  What are some of the pedagogies that law professors already use as we consider ways to update
  our syllabi for AI and how to stay ahead of the bots? My suggested answers and perspective on
  legal education are based on more than forty years as a law professor that includes teaching
  fourteen subjects and serving for nine years as vice dean and twice as acting dean. During those
  years and still counting as a full-time law professor, I have gained personal experience using a
  range of familiar pedagogies among law professors when teaching substantive courses11
  including contracts and business organizations and skills courses including negotiations and
  12
  mediation.
  
  When teaching my first substantive course early in my career, I began like many law teachers by
  using a textbook based on the original Langdell13 court case method with Socratic questioning of
  students. Shortly afterwards, I switched to problem-based14 textbooks, based on the business
  school case method. Each chapter was organized around “client problems” with the equivalent of
  a case file that can include court cases, statutes, and some documentation.
  
  After using problem-based textbooks for the majority of my career, I switched to a flipped
  classroom textbook15 about seven years ago for Contracts. The textbook is written and organized
  
