  differently than a case or problem-based textbook. In the flipped classroom16 textbook that I am using, students read in advance treatise-type readings with only a few lengthy case excerpts, watch short descriptive videos, and prepare answers to two to three problems in the textbook for classroom discussion. For classroom time, I adapted team-learning on steroids for this large enrollment course. 17 After reviewing briefly key learning points from the readings, I devote majority of class time to students working in small teams to reach an answer to each problem, that we debrief in class. At the same time as adopting team-learning, I began incorporating many formative assessments. The one summative test at the end of the semester, preferred by many teachers including me, no longer fit what we now know about student learning and the benefits of feedback during the semester. 18
  
  For teaching the skills course, I have used pedagogies that are typical for these relatively small and highly interactive courses. Each class is centered around simulations that consist of small group exercises where students negotiate with each other, mediate, or engage in client representation. Each exercise is usually introduced with preliminary readings and lectures on theory. After each exercise, we debrief in class what was learned. When reflecting back, I suspect that these teaching experiences prepared me to adapt and customize team learning for a large contracts course with a flipped classroom textbook.
  
  Through the use of this diversity of familiar pedagogies, I have experienced first-hand multiple ways for engaging students actively in the classroom. Then, the world changed in late 2022 with the wide introduction to the public of AI. 19 Since then, we have been getting acquainted with AI’s capabilities as this new phenomenon disrupts our courses.
  
  Based on this perspective and experience, I will next share an approach for incorporating AI into our syllabi.
  
  ## D. How can law professors update courses for AI now?
  
  This section suggests one approach for integrating AI now into our courses as we sort out our value-added as human teachers.
  
  After getting acquainted with AI’s capabilities and student uses, it was time to go operational in the classroom and incorporate AI into my teaching. I revisited each class with one overall question: How can the use of AI contribute to the education of my students given my goals for a particular class? To answer that question, I considered three AI applications: student uses for learning, student uses for law practice, and teacher uses for enhancing students’ education.
  
