  _1\. Uses of AI by students_
  This section focuses primarily on the risks of using AI for learning or in law practice. We have 20 already considered how students can constructively use bots for learning. We will not consider how to constructively use AI in the practice of law, although much could be discussed for teachers interested in offering more than discussing the risks generally posed by using any AI. For those interested in teaching specific law practice applications, there are ample other sources 21 that summarize the ever-changing and evolving landscape. Practice uses are increasing by the day for conducting legal research, for drafting pleadings, briefs and contracts, and for summarizing pleadings, depositions, contracts and articles.
  
  This section gives attention to three common risks that AI users should guard against: hallucinations, errors, and summarizing.
  
  At this early stage of generative AI, hallucinations have already received much embarrassing attention in the practice of law where lawyers and even a few judges have submitted briefs or issued orders that have cited court cases identified by AI that did not exist. AI can fabricate 22 answers that sound convincing. AI also can make errors, which are different than hallucinations. It can give incorrect answers, which are not made-up ones. In addition, AI can miss important details when summarizing cases, affidavits, depositions, etc. although AI can
  
