  Before students prepare for the class on the Parol Evidence Rule and Merger/NOM29 clauses, I plan to ask them to use any off-the-shelf, free bot to generate a draft merger clause and save the clause for the end of the class. After covering the lesson with class problems, I will ask students to re-read the AI-generated merger clause to assess whether anything is missing based on what they have learned. This exercise should highlight the risks of relying blindly on an AI answer along with the value of learning the materials for assessing an AI-generated answer. The exercise will reinforce the lesson that the more a student knows, the better prompts a student will be able to formulate for reducing risks of hallucinations, errors, and uninformed AI judgments and for preparing useful AI-generated drafts. Like with the battle of forms exercise, comparing a student’s understanding of the clause before and after class will enhance students’ understanding of the assigned materials too.
  
  -AI Contract Coach30 I am offering students in my Spring, 2026 first-year contracts course access to an AI Contracts Coach trained on the assigned textbook. 31 The Contracts bot is available for each student as an
  
  29 NOM stands for “no oral modification” clauses that bar modifying a contract orally. 30 _See Beth McMurtrie, The Student Bain On AI, THE CHRONICLE OF HIGHER EDUCATION (September 9, 2025) (This_ article is well worth reading in full. The article was triggered by the MIT study released in June, 2025 on possible impact of AI on the brain and learning. Based on that widely publicized study and other related studies, the article offers valuable thoughts on how bots can be effective tutors when trained and used as an aid or can undermine learning when used as a shortcut that can result in cognitive offloading, less retention and less understanding. The article highlights the benefits of a bot trained for educating students that can engage them in interactive learning through questions and feedback.
  
