  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 aid to learning, not as a shortcut. The bot does not just give answers to questions. It is designed to foster a learning dialogue between a student and bot that a student initiates by asking the bot questions. The bot replies with answers and follow-up questions to probe whether the student understood what it just learned. A student also can ask the bot to generate multiple-choice and essay questions of various degrees of difficulty. The bot will not give the student any answers, however. Instead, it will assess the student’s answer and give specific feedback regarding what the student answered correctly and missed. This teaching bot should not only enhance students’ learning but also may help them further appreciate the limitations of using an off-the-shelf, generic bot.
  
  In the written instructions for using the bot, students are informed of the different ways that it can aid learning in the course. The instructions include advice on how to formulate prompts that can optimize the benefits, warnings about the risks of using any AI platform, and illustrations of constructive and destructive uses. Each student must affirm that they have read the instructions before the student can use the Contracts Coach 32
  
  The early feedback by one student is encouraging and suggests that the bot is interacting with students in the way it was trained to teach. The student emailed me that “I’ve been using it to help me break the material apart into its simplest pieces and walk through issues step-by-step. The most positive aspect, for me, is that it doesn’t just give the answer outright but walks me through the reasoning the way you do in class. That structure has helped me focus on how to think through problems rather than just what the conclusion is. Because of that, I’ve been studying significantly more and engaging more actively with the material." (Email, dated 2/13/26, reprinted with student’s permission.)
  
  The Contracts Coach is my most ambitious use of AI in any course and should offer rich learning opportunities for students, and for me. I will be monitoring how students use the bot throughout the semester to see whether the bot stays in the teacher’s mode and whether its uses boost and not undermine comprehension and retention. My next AI article will consider what I learned
  
  The custom GPT format leverages the basic LLM content as a foundation, but on top of that foundation adds domain specific content—in this case, the full text of the course textbook. In addition to uploading the course textbook, the bot received extensive instructions (five pages of prompts) on student interactions, including instructions to give priority to the specific content over generic LLM content. These instructions dramatically improve the accuracy of the responses, consistent with the manner in which the material is taught in this specific course. The bot also is designed to engage students through, explanations, questions on the explanations, and feedback on students’ answers, what learning, and what missing. Any student attempting to use a generic LLM for the same purpose will see a stark difference in response quality.
  
  In what is likely the first rigorous study of the reliability of a bot based on an assigned law textbook, the authors concluded that the bot made too many errors when asked student-type questions for it to be ready for prime time use in a law course. See Lisa Larrimore et al., Can AI Hold Office Hours? J. LEGAL EDUC (forthcoming 2026). The Contracts Coach bot that I am offering is a much-improved one based on developments since their study. The Contracts Coach is based on Open AI 5.2 instead of an earlier 4.o version, a textbook that was chunked (broken into smaller overlapping sections for improving reliable reading by the bot), and five pages of customized prompts (instructions) designed to reduce hallucinations and engage students in a dialogue over just giving answers.
  
