  Negotiation Course These AI illustrations demonstrate the use of bots that are customized to enhance a specific class lesson.
  
  -Class Lesson: Preparing for a Negotiation When introducing negotiations, I present a negotiation map33 that tracks seven key stops in the negotiation process. The map can be valuable not only for structuring learning in the course but also as a handy reference in practice when in a rapidly unfolding negotiation. To enhance students’ learning in class exercises, I built a customized bot based on the negotiation map. The bot can be used by a student when preparing for a negotiation exercise. A student can upload a file that summarizes the dispute and then ask the bot to generate a draft negotiation plan that the student can assess and solidify when preparing for the exercise.
  
  -Class Lesson: Learning Negotiation Styles Early in the semester, I introduce the concept of negotiation styles based on classifying a choice (move) in a negotiation as either a good practice, tactic, or trick, as earlier referenced. 34 In class, students discuss an assigned article on negotiation styles and engage in exercises designed to introduce the benefits and risks of each of the three choices and how the mix of choices produces a style. By uploading the article to a bot and adding some customized instructions, the bot can help a student prepare for a class exercise or a real negotiation. A student can ask the bot to classify and analyze a choice in order to help the student assess the benefits and risks of employing the choice in a specific negotiation. 35
  
  -Class Lesson: Learning Active Listening Skills I never have sufficient time for teaching active listening skills (ALS) beyond introducing the basic and valuable concepts of summarizing, empathy, mirroring, framing, asking questions, and more. So, I built a bot based on a summary of ALS articles assigned in class to further student learning. After I introduce the skills in class, students can use the bot outside of class to practice and sharpen their skills. Either the student or bot can create a hypothetical problem, and then the student can use ALS when interacting with the bot that is performing the role of the other party. Afterwards, the bot gives feedback on how the student might have better used ALS with examples of improved ways to summarize, be empathetic, mirror, ask questions and more. The bot can also be useful for a lawyer who would like to practice constructively discussing a delicate topic with the bot before meeting the other attorney in a negotiation.
  
