  Simulations, which are central to any skills course although useful in any course, give students an opportunity to practice what they are studying. In negotiation courses, students typically participate in multiple exercises where students negotiate with each other and then debrief the experiences and any lessons. These exercises can solidify students’ understanding of the materials while helping them retain what they are learning. However, these exercises can consume considerable class time.
  
  Thanks to AI, I realized that these rich educational opportunities can be extended to after class by building bots for students to negotiate against so that students can practice on their own schedule. 36 After each bot negotiation, the bot can provide detailed feedback with hypothetical grades. I have heard from some students that negotiating with a bot requires better preparation than negotiating against a classmate because a bot knows thoroughly the facts, never forgets, and can respond quickly and in-depth.
  
  These negotiating bots also can be used in law practice when a lawyer is trying to prepare for an upcoming negotiation. A lawyer can upload information about the dispute and the other attorney to a trained bot and assign the bot the role of the other attorney. Then the lawyer can practice negotiating with the bot (like a moot) before meeting with the other attorney.
  
  Grading -Bots can grade too, 37 which might be the most attractive AI opportunity for professors. Imagine AI helping us with the most onerous part of our job. I had no plans last summer to explore AI and grading, but I inadvertently peeked into AI’s potential for offering a more time efficient and likely more reliable and robust way to grade students.
  
  I asked one of my bots to assess the success of a negotiation based on seven criteria that I typically teach students and lawyers to use for self-assessments. I was exploring AI’s capacity to give feedback so I purposely negotiated badly to see whether the bot would give me a poor assessment with helpful guidance. I was curious, because as anyone who has worked with ChatGPT knows, it can be generally nice and supportive.
  
  36 Here are two examples of customized bots that I created. One bot is labelled the Franklin Hospital Negotiation where a student can select which of the two parties the student would like to represent and then negotiate directly with the bot representing the other party. It is a simple contract dispute for physical therapy services between a hospital and a business that offers physical therapy services. Another bot, that is labelled the French Bytes Negotiation, performs as a nasty hard bargainer, a former employer who is suing a former employee performed by a student in an employment dispute. The student gains experience negotiating against an adversarial negotiator. At the end of each exercise, the student can ask the bot for feedback, and the bot will generate lengthy, specific comments with suggested grades for each of seven criteria for a successful negotiation. The student also can ask follow-up questions to further clarify any feedback. These homegrown exercises are showing great promise. For more, see _also a relatively early and informative study of negotiation simulations with a bot that is probably already outdated_ given improvements in AI since the study, ANDERSON, Negotiating with GPT-4: Digital doormat or skillful _counterpart? (SINGAPORE MANAGEMENT UNIVERSITY, YONG PUNG HOW SCH. OF L., Feb. 2025)._ 37 I am not focusing on the potential for AI to grade exams. But others are. See e.g. COPE, FRANKENREITER, HIRST, POSNER, SCHWARCZ AND THORLEY, Grading Machines: Can AI Exam-Grading Replace Law Professors?, (UNIV. OF CHI. L. SCH., COASE-SANDOR INST. FOR L. & ECON., Research Paper No. 25-35)(Dec. 2025) (Study of how LLMs performed evaluating student responses to typical law school exams.).
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026
  
  * * *
  
  I was shocked and impressed to see the bot accurately fail me based on two of the criteria and gave me Cs and Ds based on the other criteria, along with helpful, detailed feedback on what I could do better next time. These AI benefits are exciting to contemplate although I am still at the beta testing stage as I was recently reminded when a colleague mistakenly clicked the assessment prompt before beginning to negotiate. He received a B. So I suppose a bot, like we humans sometime do, can give a passing grade for just showing up.
  
  These illustrations of how to incorporate AI into our courses can be implemented now as we confront the ultimate question about our value as human teachers.
  
  ## E. What is left for human law professors to do in an AI world?
  
  Let me circle back to the beginning of the article to consider what might be our value-added as human teachers. We seem to be better than bots at teaching students to be critical thinkers and problem-solvers. 38 For law professors, this claim is encapsulated in our refrain that we teach students “to think like a lawyer.” 39
  
  To assess this claim as a human advantage, we need to consider what teaching to think like a lawyer means as a benchmark for gauging whether humans possess an edge over bots. Let me offer the following definitional description of this broad, malleable and debatable teaching goal.
  
  We teach students to be skeptical, reflective, reasoned thinkers. We teach them to read carefully, including court cases and statutes. We teach them to assess arguments including how to spot
  
  38 _See Cecilia Ka Yuk Chan & Louisa H.Y. Tsi, The AI Revolution in Education: Will AI Replace or Assist Teachers_ _in Higher Education? (May 2, 2023) (preprint), arXiv:2305.01185, [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01185](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01185) (Based on a_ literature review of articles mostly published before ChatGPT was released in November, 2022, authors identified 8 categories and 26 aspects that highlight unique skills, qualities and experiences of human teachers that AI cannot yet replicate, including social-emotional, creative, and critical-thinking contributions of educators. The authors then report on a survey they conducted of students and teachers at Hong Kong universities on whether AI will replace teachers or work alongside them. The results favor alongside in view of the unique contributions of human teachers.)
  
  For guidance on teaching with AI, see e.g. Michael Roberto, Help Students Think Critically in the Age of AI (Harv. Bus. Publ’n, Dec. 19, 2024) ( [https://hbsp.harvard.edu/inspiring-minds/enhance-critical-thinking-students-ai-](https://hbsp.harvard.edu/inspiring-minds/enhance-critical-thinking-students-ai-) assignment-strategies); Michael Roberto, Using Generative AI to Create Engaging, Customize Learning _Experiences (Harv. Bus. Impact, Webinar, Sept. 16, 2025); See also Rick Holbeck, Helping Students Develop AI_ _Prompting Skills for Critical Thinking (June 27, 2025), Faculty Focus (making the case that AI prompting can_ promote ethical reasoning, synthesis, and analysis). 39 I am using this common goal to teach students to “think like a lawyer” broadly and include a descriptive definition in the text. I recognized that not every reader will agree on how broadly or narrowly to define the expression. However, this is not the occasion for that discussion or likely debate. One earlier narrow definition distinguishes between “thinking” like a lawyer from “doing” like a lawyer as a way to foster introduction of skills and clinical courses into curriculums for teaching “doing.” At this later stage development of skills and clinical courses in law schools, I believe that this “thinking” skill can be cultivated in all courses including skills and clinical courses. See Nancy B. Rappoport, Is “Think Like a Lawyer” Really What We Want to Teach? 1 J. OF THE ASS’N LEGAL WRITING DIRECTORS (ALWD 2001 Conf. Proc.) 91 (2002). _See also Elizabeth Mertz, The Language of Law School: Learning to Think Like a Lawyer (Oxford Univ. Press,_
  
  2007. (empirical study of 1L classrooms showing how legal discourse and the case-dialogue method inculcate “thinking like a lawyer”); Cheryl B. Preston, Penée Wood Stewart & Louise R. Moulding, Teaching “Thinking Like _a Lawyer”: Metacognition and Law Students, 2014 BYU L. REV. 1053 (2015) (arguing that explicit metacognitive_ instruction helps students learn to think like lawyers and reporting supporting data). © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 issues, question assumptions, and evaluate evidence to support an argument. We teach them to
        recognize multiple perspectives including both sides of an argument and especially how to
        manage ambiguities and formulate answers in a gray world of no clear answers. We teach them
        how a small change in the facts can change an outcome. We teach them to identify multiple
        pathways toward a solution and not be afraid to experiment and fail while staying optimistic in
        search of creative possibilities.
  
  We are widely known to teach students to reply to a question with two words, “It depends.” This
  frequent reply, which can irritate others, is modeled by us in class when students ask us
  questions. Built into this common reply is an essential lesson. Thinking like a lawyer also means
  knowing what we need to know before answering a question. A single question should generate
  more questions by a lawyer before a lawyer is ready to answer a question.
  
  We teach this mode of thinking like a lawyer in the way we conduct our courses. We tell law
  students that by doing their homework and coming to class, they will learn this singular way of
  approaching issues and solving problems. But, can we still claim an advantage when Olivia and
  other intelligent bots are joining us in the classroom? Bots can be brilliant, friendly, and
  interactive with endless patience. Bots can be impressive as I saw up close when building and
  beta testing about a dozen bots this past summer. And, these AI capabilities are early stages ones.
  
  As bots become more sophisticated teachers and more used by students for learning, we need to
  give renewed attention to what we specifically do that makes it worthwhile for students to come
  to our classrooms.
  
  One starting point for figuring out our value-added can be revisiting the workhorse of the
  classroom experience, the lecture. This classic pedagogy offers an efficient way for delivering
  information in the classroom, although its use has been contracting before AI arrived as new
  40
  pedagogies have been introduced over the years. With the arrival of bots as private teachers, is
  the lecture finally dead? When students have 24-hour access to AI teachers, why should we use
  limited class time to convey information? Although I have been informally asking this question
  of other professors to inform my thinking, I found answers by AI to be especially apt and
  41
  succinct!
  
  AI replies suggest that the lecture is not dead, although its role and delivery method will evolve
  as we customize lectures for an AI-enhanced educational landscape. I will summarize a few of
  the points although I recommend anyone interested in a more thorough answer to ask and
  challenge your favorite AI chatbot.
  
  40
  See Infra Section B on What can Human Teachers Do with Familiar Pedagogies?
  41
  Professor Ann Nowak, a Touro Law colleague who also has become fascinated with AI’s potential shared with me
  
  41
  Professor Ann Nowak, a Touro Law colleague who also has become fascinated with AI’s potential shared with me
  several AI generated answers to my question on whether the arrival of bots means the death of the lecture. She
  asked ChatGPT, Claude and Co-pilot my question and it produced better answers than I was capable of formulating
  at the time (September 12, 2025).
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 not yet, I am adding). A human teacher can read the room for non-verbal cues to spot students that are struggling and offer empathy and encouragement that bots are not yet capable of doing. Lectures can be a catalyst for fostering curiosity and building a culture of learning including how to productively engage with AI. And, bringing students together in a classroom gives them the experience of participating in a community of learning by asking questions, building on others’ thoughts, and interacting and connecting with other curious humans.
  
  Claude 42 offered this prediction: “Rather than disappearing, lectures are likely to become more interactive and discussion-focused. Why spend lecture time delivering information that AI can provide more efficiently? Instead, professors can use class time for Socratic questioning, case study analysis, debate, collaborative problem-solving activities that leverage the unique value of _human interaction….”_ 43 (italics added). Co-pilot44 highly recommended a pedagogy as a complementary fit with AI, the flipped classroom. 45
  
  Another active learning pedagogy, team learning, that I favor, also can work well in an AI world, although it is not yet widely used in large classes. Team learning offers an experience that cannot yet be replicated by AI, so I have observed when using the pedagogy in my first-year contracts course over the last seven years, as earlier mentioned. I divide a class of about 50-60 students into teams of four to five for the semester. Each team member prepares answers in advance of class to two to three problems with questions. 46 In class, each team develops a single answer for each question that the team recorder enters on an electronic platform. 47 I can read the answers in real time while I roam around the classroom prodding teams with more questions. 48 Team
  
  42 For a general description of Claude, See Anthropic, Models Overview, [https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-) claude/models/overview (last visited Jan. 2, 2026) (describing Claude as a family of large language models developed by Anthropic for tasks including drafting, summarization, analysis, and question answering); See _also Anthropic, Claude 4 (2024), [https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-4](https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-4) (providing an overview of Claude’s_ capabilities and intended use cases). 43 Anthropic, Claude (Sept.17, 2025), [https://www.anthropic.com](https://www.anthropic.com/). 44 For a general description of Microsoft Copilot, See Microsoft, Copilot: Your AI _Companion, [https://copilot.microsoft.com/](https://copilot.microsoft.com/) (last visited Jan. 2, 2026) (describing Copilot as Microsoft’s suite of_ artificial intelligence assistants); See also Microsoft, Microsoft 365 Copilot, [https://www.microsoft.com/en-](https://www.microsoft.com/en-) us/microsoft-365-copilot (last visited Jan. 2, 2026) (explaining the integration of Copilot into workplace productivity applications such as Word, Outlook, and Excel). 45 Microsoft, Co-Pilot (Sept. 17, 2025), [https://copilot.microsoft.com](https://copilot.microsoft.com/). 46 The trained contracts bot that I offer the students has been instructed to not offer answers to any of the assigned problems in the textbook, although I have heard that some students have tried to elicit answers from the bot without success. 47 When I first started using team-learning in Contracts with Professor Jack Graves (we taught separate classes while experimenting jointly with different active-learning pedagogies), the teams initially shared and displayed their answers on individual whiteboards held above their heads that we could observe. Professor Graves purchased boxes of small whiteboards with markers and mini-erasures for our use. Then Professor Graves discovered a team-learning electronic platform that I now use that permits me to see students’ answers as they are entered and can be saved for later review. See Intedashboard at [https://www.intedashboard.com/](https://www.intedashboard.com/) 48 Here is an example of the team-learning pedagogy and benefits from my contracts class that cannot be replicated by AI: This past semester (Fall, 2025), student teams were working on a problem that raised a firm offer issue- whether the offeror’s offer was a firm one under the UCC when it stated it would keep the offer open for 20 days. As I wandered around the room prodding teams with questions, I asked one team of four students whether the offer met the requirements of the UCC as the team was preparing an answer to enter in the electronic platform. Two of the students replied confidently that it was a firm offer because the offeror “assured” it would stay open (one of the requirements). I then asked the other two students whether they agreed, and one reluctantly disagreed, joined by the
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 learning engages almost every student in a large class, which other pedagogies cannot achieve. As a bonus, students learn to discuss ideas and collaborate that are valuable skills for most law practice areas and life.
  
  These active learning pedagogies have in common the capacity to draw students directly into energetic and intensive exchanges for learning that bots cannot yet spark. This list offers a segue into the good news. These pedagogies are already familiar and tested ones used by law professors. By customizing them for our new world, we can teach thinking like a lawyer and 49 much more in ways bots cannot yet do, at least not do as well as humans, for now.
  
  **Conclusion** The AI onslaught forces us to reflect on our value as human teachers in the classroom, especially when students are already incorporating AI into their education. We need to learn to teach differently to students who are learning differently.
  
  For us to stay relevant and effective as teachers, I am suggesting three questions to contemplate going forward: (1) How can we teach responsible use of AI not only for professional practice but also for studying and class preparation? (2) How can we use bots in our classes to enhance our 50 teaching? (3) How can we stay ahead of the bots by doing what bots cannot do?
  
  The presence of AI is our reality as we figure out how to harness AI while staying one step ahead. My conclusion changed during the drafting of this article. I first thought that bots would 51 take over our jobs. Then, I thought bots would simply be helpful teaching assistants. Now I view bots as co-teachers that will make us better teachers by pressing us to take a fresh look at an old and familiar question: What are the best pedagogies for convincing students to spend their
  
  fourth student. The two students read the language in the offer as not giving “adequate assurances,” which prompted the two confident students to re-read the offer by parsing the exact wording. The disagreement triggered a discussion among the four students that AI could not foster as the students engaged in a close reading of the applicable UCC provision and the wording of the offer. The disagreement also guided my debriefing of the problem with the full class where I referenced the team discussion.
  
  This is an example of a value-added pedagogy that we humans can bring to the classroom.
  
  49 For now? How much longer will humans maintain this advantage is an interesting question for another paper and more studies. In a recent article in the Chronicle of Higher Education, the author suggests that AI is already intruding into our territory of strengths when comparing two studies, both of which were conducted at Harvard University. In a pre-AI experiment, one study showed that students learning from lectures thought they understood the lessons from listening although their test scores were lower than test scores for students who were learning in an active learning environment. The authors viewed hearing lectures as similar to learning from bots. These results presumably are not surprising. But, in a later AI experiment, a study showed that students who used a customized AI tutor that was carefully designed and rigorously tested did better in a physics lesson than students did when engaged in a human’s active-learning classroom. And this bot was an early-stage one, I should point out. See Beth McMurtrie, The Student Bain On AI The Chronicle of Higher Education (September 9, 2025).
  
  50 For an excellent article that surveys in depth multiple ways for possibly incorporating AI into legal education, see John Bliss, Teaching Law in the Age of Generative AI, 64 JURIMETRICS J. 111, 132, 136, 141, 149 151 (considers whether or not AI tutors (bots) can teach thinking like a lawyer and more sophisticated reasoning skills). Of course, this is what AI chatboxes would like us to think when they try to comfort us by saying that bots can only assist us humans.
  
  © [Habramson@tourolaw.edu](mailto:Habramson@tourolaw.edu) Draft, 2/23/2026 precious time in our classrooms? Figuring that out means figuring out how to best combine learning opportunities offered by AI with learning opportunities that can only be offered by humans. How best to do that will be a works-in-progress with many unknowns. That is the exciting, challenging, and unsettling journey ahead of us.
  
  For anyone interested in any predictions, as colleagues have pressed me to make, this article avoids the ultimate question whether AI will replace us. In this article, I humbly offer a plan for staying agile and ahead of the bots as we continue our own learning. As the bots get better, we must adapt our pedagogies to stay relevant and effective as human teachers in an AI world. This is our new reality.
  
