e way that I read it was that she is able to sort of like blend her experience working on these tech projects with practice, which in a lot of cases in firms, it's like you have to choose a path and then you're on it. And it it one of the things that repels people from working in firms and it doesn't sound like that's what happened here. So what do you think a firm or an organization because you are supporting an entire organization in this transformation can do to support these efforts in addition to culture? Culture is kind of like a foundational requirement, right? If you have a culture of um space to learn, encouragement to learn, space to innovate, encouragement to to innovate, that matters. Um I mean I've I've said this in lots of maybe I've said it too much on this podcast, but I think you you need everybody in. I really agree with the lawyers who don't like when you have to choose a path and I I think that is still somewhat common. Like you can choose to be on the kind of AI tech side of your firm, the ops side or you can be on the substantive side. I think that's a huge mistake. you know, I think it's like missing opportunities to see problems and solutions. Um, because everybody just sees what's in their lane instead of the places where their lanes intersect. Um, so I think you really need everybody in I I've said this a million times, has to be top down, bottom up. I think you're whoever's the managing partner of your firm has to be like vibe coding herself and showing everybody where she's like succeeding and where she's not. um getting people excited about it. Um then I think you have to um give people like time and space to actually do it, not just say we want everybody doing it. And there's lots of different ways firms can do that because they have, you know, ways to structure that into um your expectations that I actually think they that that's an advantage they have. Um, and you probably have to put some real resources to it, which is, you know, R&D