  ## SPONSORED CONTENT
  
  # Reimagining Legal Education in the Age of Artificial Intelligence
  
  By Stacy Leeds, Willard H. Pedrick, Dean at ASU Law, and Sean Harrington, Director of the AI and Legal Tech Studio at ASU Law
  
  Legal education is at an inflection point.
  
  [Artificial intelligence](https://law.asu.edu/ai) is no longer a distant disruption to the legal profession. It is already reshaping how lawyers research, draft, negotiate, advise clients and deliver services. Yet legal education has struggled to respond in a way that is both principled and practical. Too often, the conversation oscillates between fear-driven restrictions and surface-level experimentation.
  
  At the Sandra Day O’Connor College of Law at Arizona State University, we believe that moment has passed. The real question is not whether artificial intelligence belongs in legal education, but whether law schools are prepared to redesign their institutions to reflect the profession their graduates will enter and while being nimble to forecast and adapt to inevitable future innovations.
  
  We decided it was time to move from isolated AI pilots to an integrated, institution-wide approach. On April 15 at 9 a.m. MST, ASU Law will host the virtual webinar “Reimagining Legal Education in the Age of Artificial Intelligence.” In this session, we will discuss how artificial intelligence is reshaping legal research, drafting and service delivery, and how legal education must evolve to prepare the next generation of attorneys through curriculum integration and applied learning. [Register now for the webinar](http://asu.law/aiwebinar).
  
  **Building AI fluency from day one**
  
  Our strategy begins before students ever step into a classroom. All incoming JD students receive mandatory training during orientation on the ethical, professional and practical use of artificial intelligence. This foundation is critical. AI literacy is not about learning prompts or shortcuts; it is about understanding how these systems function, where they fail, how bias can be embedded and what professional responsibility demands in an AI-enabled practice.
  
  From there, AI is embedded across the curriculum rather than confined to a single course or elective. Faculty integrate AI tools into classes to deepen learning, sharpen analysis and reinforce judgment. Importantly, our students are not just using AI systems; they are learning how to build, evaluate and responsibly deploy them.
  
  Through hands-on training in prompt design, model evaluation and application development, students gain systems-level fluency that reflects how legal work is actually evolving. Employers increasingly expect new graduates to understand not only how to generate AI output, but how to assess its reliability and supervise its use. We have implemented interactive and gamified learning techniques using a Socratic-style AI quizbot that challenges students to test their reasoning in real time while maintaining academic rigor.
  
  This work extends beyond teaching. We have operationalized AI thoughtfully across law school administration, academic support and student success programs, recognizing that institutional practices shape student learning as much as syllabi do. We are actively exploring AI-enabled tools to support bar preparation and academic success, focusing on personalization, formative feedback and early identification of areas where students may benefit from additional support.
  
  We are also piloting a proprietary AI learning system within our online JD, developed by Ph.D.-trained AI engineers. While the technology remains in development, its purpose is clear: to enhance learning through responsible, research-driven innovation while keeping human judgment and creativity firmly at the center.
  
  **From theory to applied impact**
  
  AI competence cannot remain theoretical. Law students must graduate with experience applying these tools in real-world contexts, guided by ethical constraints and professional norms.
  
  This fall, students in ASU Law’s [AI and Legal Tech Studio](https://law.asu.edu/newsroom/asu-law-appoints-director-ai-and-legal-tech-studio-advancing-its-initiative-reimagine) will pursue one of two applied pathways. One track focuses on building AI-enabled legal applications, exposing students to product development, legal workflows and interdisciplinary collaboration with technologists and engineers. Students learn to move from concept to deployment, evaluating performance, mitigating risk and refining systems in real-world contexts. The second centers on public service, where students apply AI tools to address access-to-justice challenges and community needs.
  
  For our online JD students, this applied emphasis includes a pro bono AI project launching this spring, enabling students to work directly with public interest partners and real clients. These experiences reinforce a core principle: artificial intelligence should expand access to legal services and improve quality. These tools cannot be limited to merely increasing the efficiency and reducing the costs for those already well served and well resourced.
  
  **Credentialing for a changing profession**
  
  The legal profession is signaling a clear need for graduates who can engage with AI thoughtfully, ethically and competently. In response, ASU Law is developing a certification program in AI and legal technology for upper-level JD students, alumni and practicing lawyers.
  
  This credentialing initiative reflects a broader commitment to lifelong learning. AI will not stand still, and legal education cannot either. By extending structured training to law firms and alumni, we aim to support responsible adoption across the profession while strengthening the connection between legal education and legal practice.
  
  Our student opportunities outside of the classroom and studio will include national and international AI conferences, competitions and convenings. Student voices are critically important in the conversations that shape the future of legal services.
  
  These efforts are reinforced by the College’s longstanding leadership through the [Center for Law, Science and Innovation](https://law.asu.edu/centers/law-science-innovation), which for more than three decades has shaped governance conversations at the intersection of law and emerging technology. Long before AI became a focal point in legal education, the Center established itself as a national benchmark in areas such as biotechnology, intellectual property and technology regulation. Our leadership in AI is not a sudden pivot, but the continuation of a deeply rooted institutional commitment to anticipating how technological change transforms legal systems and professional responsibility.
  
  Today, the Center’s work extends beyond AI alone, examining its convergence with quantum computing, autonomous systems and commercial space activity — and the regulatory and ethical challenges that follow. With more than 50 law, science and technology courses and robust faculty research in artificial intelligence and governance, ASU Law’s approach reflects not only curricular innovation but sustained national leadership. The Studio, housed within the Center, translates that foundation into applied student experience.
  
  Together, these efforts make clear that AI literacy is no longer a niche skill; it is rapidly becoming a professional baseline.
  
  **A call for collective leadership**
  
  If legal education is to remain worthy of the profession it serves, we must be willing to reimagine it now. Through the integration of applied AI training, institutional leadership in technology governance and the launch of our AI-enabled online JD focused on public service and legal deserts, we are not simply adapting to change. We are helping shape the future of the legal profession itself.
  
